---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import AceCount from '~/islands/AceCount';
import AceFit from '~/islands/AceFit';
import AcePace from '~/islands/AcePace';
import pace from '~/data/models/ace-pace-cu.json';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import type { Checkpoint, Dataset } from '~/content/schemas';
import { LAB_MAX_RATTLE, LAB_MAX_STRAIN, LAB_RADIAL, LAB_SPEC } from '~/lib/models/ace-fit';

const cu = (await getEntry('checkpoints', 'pace-cu-2021'))!.data as Checkpoint;
const cuData = (await getEntry('datasets', 'cu-pace-pbe'))!.data as Dataset;
const s = pace.summary;

const countAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Construction', value: 'Every coupling tree ((l₁ l₂)L₁ l₃)L₂ … → 0 of the sorted tuple, from Clebsch–Gordan coefficients (Racah’s formula, Condon–Shortley phase) mapped to the real harmonics of the Atlas by a unitary matrix computed by quadrature. Real and imaginary parts of each tree are candidates.' },
  { term: 'Rank test', value: 'Candidates evaluated on 3 × (candidates) + 6 random environments; Gram–Schmidt keeps one when it adds a direction larger than 10⁻⁷ of its norm. This is Dusson et al.’s linear-algebra step, done with random samples instead of an SVD of the coefficient matrix.' },
  { term: 'Characters', value: 'Eq. (5) integrated with a midpoint rule in the rotation angle, exact because the integrand is a cosine polynomial of known degree.' },
  { term: 'Growth curve', value: 'One species, n ≥ 1, Σ_t (n_t + l_t) ≤ D for every order (w_L = 1), Σl even. Dashed lines count the distinct products Π A over m before coupling, which is what an A-basis evaluator multiplies.' },
];
const fitAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Reference', value: 'Stillinger–Weber Si (LAMMPS Si.sw) or Tersoff Si(B) (LAMMPS Si.tersoff, Tersoff 1988), so every energy is exact. Forces by central differences of the total energy (h = 10⁻⁴ Å).' },
  { term: 'Data', value: `8-atom diamond cells, isotropic strain uniform in ±${100 * LAB_MAX_STRAIN} %, Gaussian rattle with amplitude uniform in [0, ${LAB_MAX_RATTLE}] Å per component; 16 test cells drawn the same way with another seed. Fixed seeds.` },
  { term: 'Basis', value: `Linear ACE, one species: ν ≤ ${LAB_SPEC.numax}, n ≤ ${LAB_SPEC.nmax}, l ≤ ${LAB_SPEC.lmax}, Σ(n + l) ≤ ${LAB_SPEC.degree.join(', ')} for ν = 1 … ${LAB_SPEC.numax}; ChebExpCos radial functions with λ = ${LAB_RADIAL.lambda}, r_c = ${LAB_RADIAL.rc} Å (no learned radial coefficients). Each grid cell is the subset with ν and l at most the values shown.` },
  { term: 'Fit', value: 'Least squares on energies per atom and force components with equal weight (eV/atom and eV/Å), ridge λ relative to unit-RMS columns, plus a constant per atom. Normal equations, Cholesky.' },
  { term: 'Curves', value: 'Dimer: E(r) − 2E_atom of two atoms in vacuum; the reference is the potential’s pair term. Strain: perfect diamond. Core repulsion, when added, is pacemaker’s pre·exp(−λr²)/r times the cosine envelope with the documentation example’s pre = 100 eV·Å, λ = 5 Å⁻², added after the fit (it is below 10⁻⁵ eV per atom on every training cell).' },
  { term: 'Not shown', value: 'Not pacemaker or ACEpotentials.jl defaults: no learned radial functions, no smoothness prior, no nonlinear embedding, a tiny cell. Computed in your browser in a Web Worker in a few seconds.' },
];
const paceAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Potential', value: `${s.total_functions} basis functions in ${cu.version}, md5 ${pace.source.md5.slice(0, 8)}…, from Zenodo ${pace.source.doi} (CC-BY-4.0).` },
  { term: 'Evaluator', value: `${pace.source.evaluator}, run by atlas_pipeline.ace_pace.` },
  { term: 'Split', value: 'The file evaluated three more times: with every density-2 coefficient set to zero (ρ₁ + core), with every density-1 coefficient zero (F(ρ₂) + core), with both zero (core). The three parts add up to the total within 10⁻¹³ eV.' },
  { term: 'Structures', value: 'Dimer in a 40 Å box without periodicity; fcc and bcc cubic cells and ideal hcp (c/a = √(8/3)) on 25 volumes each.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Atomic cluster expansion"
  description="The atomic cluster expansion (ACE): a complete, body-ordered basis of the site energy built from products of density projections. Its basis counted two ways and checked against Dusson et al., a linear ACE fitted live to Stillinger–Weber and Tersoff silicon, and the released PACE copper potential split into its linear and square-root parts."
>
  <article class="page-grid model-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/models/">Models</a> · Model family</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Atomic cluster expansion</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The atomic cluster expansion (ACE) writes an atom’s energy in a basis that can represent any smooth function of its
        neighbours, ordered by how many neighbours each term involves <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Theorem 8" />. Its trick
        is the order of operations: project the neighbour density on one-particle functions first, then multiply, so that a
        term coupling ν neighbours costs a single pass over them <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Eqs. (2)–(5)" />. SOAP,
        the bispectrum and moment tensors are all subsets or re-expressions of the same space
        <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="App. B" />.
      </p>
      <dl class="glance">
        <div><dt>Symmetry</dt><dd>Exact invariance: Clebsch–Gordan-coupled products</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Locality</dt><dd>Strictly local: 5.5–7.4 Å in the PACE potentials</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Regression</dt><dd>Linear in the coefficients, or a fixed nonlinear function of a few linear expansions</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Body order</dt><dd>Any: ν products give (ν + 1)-body terms</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Cost</dt><dd>Linear in neighbours; 0.32 ms/atom per force call for the Cu PACE</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Introduced</dt><dd>PRB 2019; PACE and pacemaker 2021–22; ACEpotentials.jl 2023</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/ICAMS/python-ace">pacemaker</a> (ASL), <a href="https://github.com/ACEsuit/ACEpotentials.jl">ACEpotentials.jl</a> (MIT), LAMMPS ML-PACE</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="rule-note">
        <Badge label="schematic" /> The <strong>architecture</strong> is a basis; ACE potentials are fitted per system. One
        released checkpoint, the PACE copper potential, is evaluated in Figure 3. The message-passing descendant is on the
        <a href="/models/grace/">GRACE page</a>, which also shows the density trick’s self-interaction terms.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="dens-h">
      <h2 id="dens-h" class="h-section">Densities, then products</h2>
      <p>
        The neighbours of atom <i>i</i> are a density ρ<sub><i>i</i></sub>(<b>r</b>) = Σ<sub><i>j</i></sub> δ(<b>r</b> − <b>r</b><sub><i>ji</i></sub>).
        ACE projects it on one-particle functions, a radial function times a spherical harmonic, to get the atomic base
        <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Eqs. (2), (3), (10), (11)" /><Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Eq. (4.2)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`A_{i,nlm} = \sum_{j} R_{nl}(r_{ji})\, Y_{lm}(\hat{\mathbf r}_{ji})`} />
      <p>
        and multiplies ν of them. The product of sums is the sum over all ν-tuples of neighbours of a ν-neighbour function, so a
        (ν + 1)-body term costs one pass over the neighbours and ν − 1 multiplications instead of a loop over all tuples
        <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Eqs. (4)–(5), (12)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`\mathbf A_{i,\mathbf{nlm}} = \prod_{t=1}^{\nu} A_{i,n_t l_t m_t} = \sum_{j_1,\dots,j_\nu}\ \prod_{t=1}^{\nu} R_{n_t l_t}(r_{j_t i})\,Y_{l_t m_t}(\hat{\mathbf r}_{j_t i})`} />
      <p>
        The sum includes tuples in which a neighbour repeats. Those terms have fewer distinct neighbours, so they are lower-order
        functions; ACE keeps them and lets the lower orders absorb them rather than subtracting them
        <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Eq. (4.4)" />. The products turn with the atom’s surroundings. A fixed linear combination
        over the <i>m</i>’s, the generalised Clebsch–Gordan coupling <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Eq. (13) and after" />,
        gives functions that do not:
      </p>
      <Math display number="3" tex={String.raw`B_{i,\mathbf{nl}k} = \sum_{\mathbf m} \mathcal C^{\mathbf{nl}}_{k,\mathbf m}\ \mathbf A_{i,\mathbf{nlm}}, \qquad E_i = \sum_{\nu}\sum_{\mathbf{nl}k} c_{\mathbf{nl}k}\, B_{i,\mathbf{nl}k}`} />
      <p>
        and the energy is linear in the <i>c</i>’s (Eq. 6 of the PACE paper). Because <i>B</i> = 𝒞<b>A</b>, the fitted coefficients can
        be pushed through the coupling once, <i>c̃</i> = 𝒞<sup>T</sup><i>c</i>, and the potential evaluated directly on the products;
        this is the “ctilde” basis stored in PACE files <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. III.1.1" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="count-h">
      <h2 id="count-h" class="h-section">How many invariants?</h2>
      <p>
        For a tuple of degrees <b>l</b> = (<i>l</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>l</i><sub>ν</sub>) the couplings are the ways of adding the angular
        momenta to zero: couple <i>l</i><sub>1</sub> and <i>l</i><sub>2</sub> to some <i>L</i><sub>1</sub>, that with <i>l</i><sub>3</sub>
        to <i>L</i><sub>2</sub>, and so on, each step obeying the triangle rule. With every (<i>n</i>, <i>l</i>) different, each such tree is
        a new invariant. When labels repeat, the product <b>A</b> is symmetric in the repeated factors, and trees that differ by
        exchanging them give the same function, so fewer survive <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Sec. 3.5, Table 1" />. Dusson et
        al. remove the dependences numerically, from the rank of a matrix built from the couplings
        <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Lemma 2, Eq. (3.13)" />. The count can also be written down in closed form. Group the
        tuple into distinct labels with multiplicities <i>k</i>; the products then span the representation
        ⊗<sub>groups</sub> Sym<sup><i>k</i></sup>(<i>V</i><sub><i>l</i></sub>) of the rotation group, and the number of invariants in a
        representation is the average of its character over rotations. Characters depend only on the rotation angle θ, the Haar
        measure in θ is (1 − cos θ)/π on [0, π], <i>V</i><sub><i>l</i></sub> has character χ<sub><i>l</i></sub>(θ) = sin((<i>l</i> + ½)θ)/sin(θ/2), and
        the character of a symmetric power follows from Newton’s identity for complete symmetric polynomials. Inversion multiplies
        the whole product by (−1)<sup>Σ<i>l</i></sup>, so for O(3)
      </p>
      <Math display number="4" tex={String.raw`h_k(\theta) = \frac1k \sum_{q=1}^{k} \chi_l(q\theta)\, h_{k-q}(\theta), \quad h_0 = 1, \qquad \chi_l(\theta) = \frac{\sin\big((l+\tfrac12)\theta\big)}{\sin(\theta/2)}`} />
      <Math display number="5" tex={String.raw`\#\mathrm{RPI}(\mathbf n, \mathbf l) = \frac{1 + (-1)^{\sum_t l_t}}{2}\ \frac1\pi\int_0^\pi (1-\cos\theta) \prod_{\text{groups}} h_{k}^{(l)}(\theta)\, d\theta`} />
      <p>
        With all labels distinct every <i>h</i> is a plain χ<sub><i>l</i></sub>, and Eq. (5) counts the coupling trees: the number Dusson et
        al. call #RI. Figure 1 builds the invariants both ways, the trees with a rank test and the integral, and compares them with
        the published table.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Coupling, symmetry and the size of a complete basis" labels={['live-calculation', 'reproduced']} number={1} id="fig-count" framed={false}>
        <AceCount client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: choose a correlation (ν labels (<i>n</i>, <i>l</i>)) or one of Dusson et al.’s rows. The numbers follow the construction:
          products, coupling trees, rotation invariants if every <i>n</i> differed, and the independent functions that survive the
          rank test, with the character prediction beside them. Every row of the paper’s Table 1 is reproduced, and each function
          passes a rotation test. Right: the size of the complete basis against total degree, per order. A truncated ACE is a
          choice of where to cut this growth.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={countAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Table 1 and the rank construction of <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Sec. 3.3–3.5, Table 1, App. A.3" />. Computed in your browser by <code>src/lib/models/ace.ts</code>; tests in <code>ace.test.ts</code> check every Table 1 entry both ways, Clebsch–Gordan orthonormality, rotation, reflection and permutation invariance, and exact derivatives.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="sw-h" id="sw">
      <h2 id="sw-h" class="h-section">Stillinger–Weber is an ACE with ν = 2</h2>
      <p>
        Body order is a property of the target as well as of the model, and a classical potential shows it exactly. Stillinger–Weber
        silicon has a pair term φ<sub>2</sub> and a three-body term centred on each atom, with <i>s</i>(<i>r</i>) = exp(γσ/(<i>r</i> − <i>a</i>σ))
        and the tetrahedral angle preferred (LAMMPS <code>pair_style sw</code> with the parameters of <Cite id="stillinger1985sw" />):
      </p>
      <Math display number="6" tex={String.raw`E_i = \tfrac12 \sum_j \phi_2(r_{ij}) + \lambda\varepsilon \sum_{j<k} s(r_{ij})\, s(r_{ik}) \left(\cos\theta_{jik} + \tfrac13\right)^2`} />
      <p>
        Write the angular factor in Legendre polynomials, (<i>c</i> + ⅓)² = ⁴⁄₉ <i>P</i><sub>0</sub>(<i>c</i>) + ⅔ <i>P</i><sub>1</sub>(<i>c</i>) + ⅔ <i>P</i><sub>2</sub>(<i>c</i>)
        (check: 16/9 at <i>c</i> = 1, zero at <i>c</i> = −⅓), use the addition theorem <i>P</i><sub><i>l</i></sub>(<b>r̂</b><sub><i>j</i></sub>·<b>r̂</b><sub><i>k</i></sub>) = 4π/(2<i>l</i> + 1) Σ<sub><i>m</i></sub> <i>Y</i><sub><i>lm</i></sub>(<b>r̂</b><sub><i>j</i></sub>)<i>Y</i><sub><i>lm</i></sub>(<b>r̂</b><sub><i>k</i></sub>),
        and replace Σ<sub><i>j</i>&lt;<i>k</i></sub> by half the full double sum minus its <i>j</i> = <i>k</i> terms, where the angle is zero and the factor is 16/9:
      </p>
      <Math display number="7" tex={String.raw`E_i = \sum_j \Big[\tfrac12\phi_2(r_{ij}) - \tfrac89\lambda\varepsilon\, s(r_{ij})^2\Big] + \frac{\lambda\varepsilon}{2} \sum_{l=0}^{2} a_l\,\frac{4\pi}{2l+1} \sum_{m} A_{lm}^2, \qquad A_{lm} = \sum_j s(r_{ij})\,Y_{lm}(\hat{\mathbf r}_{ij}),\ \ a = \big(\tfrac49, \tfrac23, \tfrac23\big)`} />
      <p>
        The first sum is a ν = 1 function and the second is made of ν = 2 invariants with <i>l</i> ≤ 2 and one radial function. So a
        linear ACE with ν ≤ 2, <i>l</i> ≤ 2 and radial functions able to represent <i>s</i>, <i>s</i>² and φ<sub>2</sub> reproduces
        Stillinger–Weber exactly; with ν = 1 or <i>l</i> ≤ 1 it cannot, whatever the data. The <i>j</i> = <i>k</i> terms are exactly
        the self-interactions of Eq. (2), landing in the order below. Tersoff’s potential is different: its bond order
        (1 + βⁿζⁿ)<sup>−1/2<i>n</i></sup> with <i>n</i> ≈ 23 is a steep function of a sum over all other neighbours, so expanded in body
        order it has terms of every order (LAMMPS <code>pair_style tersoff</code>, <Cite id="tersoff1988new" />).
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Fitting a linear ACE to two classical silicon potentials" labels={['live-calculation', 'toy-model']} number={2} id="fig-fit" framed={false}>
        <AceFit client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          One feature computation, sixteen fits: every combination of maximum body order and <i>l</i><sub>max</sub> is a subset of the
          same basis. For Stillinger–Weber the error collapses where Eq. (7) says it must, at ν = 2 and <i>l</i> = 2; the remainder is
          the radial basis approximating <i>s</i>(<i>r</i>). For Tersoff it falls slowly with ν, and the larger bases fit the training
          cells better than the test cells. In 3D: one test cell with the reference and ACE forces. Below: two configurations no
          training cell contains.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={fitAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Derivation above (Eq. 7). Basis <code>src/lib/models/ace.ts</code>; references, features, fit and curves <code>src/lib/models/ace-fit.ts</code> and <code>src/lib/physics/tersoff.ts</code>, tested in <code>ace-fit.test.ts</code> (Stillinger–Weber falls by several times from <i>l</i> ≤ 1 to <i>l</i> ≤ 2 at ν = 2 and is not represented at ν = 1; force features against finite differences; Tersoff and SW diamond energies). Radial functions as pacemaker’s ChebExpCos (<code>ace_radial.cpp</code>), core repulsion as its <code>radcore</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="emb-h">
      <h2 id="emb-h" class="h-section">Nonlinear embedding: PACE’s square root</h2>
      <p>
        A linear ACE needs high orders when the target depends nonlinearly on a sum over neighbours, as Tersoff does. The
        tight-binding second-moment picture of metals, the basis of Finnis–Sinclair and embedded-atom potentials, has that form: the
        bonding energy grows as the square root of a pair density. PACE therefore allows the energy to be a fixed function of several
        ACE expansions φ<sup>(<i>p</i>)</sup>, each linear in its own coefficients <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Eqs. (6)–(8)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="8" tex={String.raw`E_i = \varphi_i^{(1)} - \sqrt{\varphi_i^{(2)}}, \qquad \varphi_i^{(p)} = \sum_{\mathbf{nlm}} \tilde c^{(p)}_{\mathbf{nlm}}\, \mathbf A_{i,\mathbf{nlm}}`} />
      <p>
        In the released files the square root is pacemaker’s <code>FinnisSinclairShiftedScaled</code> embedding with <i>m</i> = ½, smoothed
        at zero and carrying the sign of ρ (python-ace, <code>ace_abstract_basis.cpp</code>):
      </p>
      <Math display number="9" tex={String.raw`F(\rho) = \operatorname{sign}(\rho)\Big(\sqrt{\tfrac14 e^{-|\rho|} + |\rho|} - \tfrac12 e^{-|\rho|}\Big), \qquad F(0) = 0,\quad F'(0) = \tfrac54,\quad F(\rho) \to \operatorname{sign}(\rho)\sqrt{|\rho|}`} />
      <p>
        Short distances are guarded by a separate core repulsion, <i>pre</i>·exp(−λ<i>r</i>²)/<i>r</i> per pair times the cosine
        envelope (<code>ace_radial.cpp</code>, <code>radcore</code>), with an optional cut on a core density
        <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Suppl. Methods, Eqs. (19)–(21)" />. For copper this form matters because the filled
        <i>d</i> shell makes angular terms small in the bulk, so the paper starts from the Finnis–Sinclair form and lets both densities
        include many-atom terms; silicon was fitted as a linear ACE with 6,827 functions up to ν = 4
        <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. I; Sec. II.2" />. Figure 3 opens the released copper potential.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Inside the released PACE copper potential" labels={['precomputed']} number={3} id="fig-pace" framed={false}>
        <AcePace client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: the potential’s basis by order. Most functions are three- and four-body (ν = 2, 3) with <i>l</i> up to 6; a handful reach
          ν = 6 with <i>l</i> ≤ 1. Right: the Cu₂ dimer energy split into the linear density ρ₁, the embedded density F(ρ₂) and the core
          repulsion, and the E(V) curves of three crystal structures. The split is exact (the parts add to the total), but neither
          density is a physical quantity: both are fitted. At the dimer’s minimum F(ρ₂) carries most of the binding, and the short-range wall comes from ρ₁, not from the core term.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={paceAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source"><Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. II.2; Suppl. Methods Eqs. (15)–(22)" />; file from <a href={pace.source.url}>Zenodo {pace.source.doi}</a>. Parsed and evaluated by <code>pipeline/atlas_pipeline/ace_pace.py</code> (tests in <code>pipeline/tests/test_ace_pace.py</code>: parsing, density zeroing, embedding and core-repulsion closed forms, closure of the split, fcc &lt; hcp &lt; bcc).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="fitp-h">
      <h2 id="fitp-h" class="h-section">Choosing and fitting a basis</h2>
      <p>
        A complete basis must be cut somewhere. ACEpotentials.jl keeps the functions whose total degree is within a budget per order,
        and stabilises the least-squares fit with a prior that penalises rough functions <Cite id="witt2023acepotentials" locator="Eqs. (9), (10), (12)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="10" tex={String.raw`\sum_{t=1}^{\nu} \big(n_t + w_L\, l_t\big) \le D_\nu, \qquad \min_{c}\ \big\| W (y - \mathsf A c) \big\|^2 + \lambda \big\| \Gamma c \big\|^2, \quad \Gamma_{kk} = \sum_t n_t^{p} + w_L\, l_t^{p}`} />
      <p>
        with <i>p</i> = 4 by default; the current code weights <i>l</i> by 1/<i>w</i><sub>l</sub> with <i>w</i><sub>l</sub> = ⅔ instead
        (<code>smoothness_priors.jl</code> at v0.10.2). pacemaker fits learned radial functions and the nonlinear embedding by
        minimising (1 − κ) times the energy error plus κ times the force error, with ℓ1, ℓ2 and radial-smoothness penalties, and can
        grow the basis in steps (“ladder” fitting) (python-ace <code>pyacefit.py</code>; <Cite id="bochkarev2022pacemaker" />). For
        a linear model the extrapolation grade of MTP carries over directly: with an active set of atomic environments chosen by MaxVol,
        γ<sub><i>i</i></sub> = max |<b>B</b><sub><i>i</i></sub> · <i>A</i><sub>active</sub><sup>−1</sup>|, and the derivatives with respect to
        the coefficients replace <b>B</b> for nonlinear ACE <Cite id="lysogorskiy2023active" locator="Eqs. (12)–(13)" /> (the method is
        shown live on the <a href="/models/mtp/#fig-si">MTP page</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="maths-h">
      <h2 id="maths-h" class="h-section">The mathematics at three depths</h2>
      <div class="depths">
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="ace-int" checked />
        <label for="ace-int">Intuition</label>
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="ace-tech" />
        <label for="ace-tech">Technical</label>
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="ace-res" />
        <label for="ace-res">Research</label>
        <div class="depth depth-int">
          <p>
            Describe the neighbours of an atom by how much of them lies along each direction at each distance: a handful of numbers per
            atom. Multiply those numbers in pairs, triples and so on, and average away the orientation. Pairs see angles between two
            neighbours, triples see the shape of three, and so on: with enough of them, any arrangement can be told apart from any
            other, and the energy is a weighted sum. Counting the distinct combinations is a question about symmetry with an exact
            answer.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="depth depth-tech">
          <p>
            One-particle basis <i>R</i><sub><i>nl</i></sub>(<i>r</i>)<i>Y</i><sub><i>lm</i></sub>(<b>r̂</b>), density projections <i>A</i><sub><i>nlm</i></sub> (Eq. 1),
            products of ν projections (Eq. 2), and O(3) invariants by generalised Clebsch–Gordan coupling (Eq. 3), with dependences from
            repeated labels removed by a rank computation and counted by Eq. (5). Forces are exact derivatives through <i>A</i>, one pass
            over the neighbours; the recursive evaluator builds high-order products from pairs of lower-order ones, which cut the Si
            potential’s cost from 1.84 to 0.80 ms/atom <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. III.3" />. Multicomponent systems add
            species to every index, and the number of functions grows with the number of species combinations.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="depth depth-res">
          <p>
            The invariant basis of all orders is linearly independent and complete in the smooth symmetric functions of the neighbourhood
            <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Theorems 6, 8" />, and SOAP, SNAP and MTP are subsets or re-expansions of it
            <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="App. B.2–B.4" />. Completeness says nothing about how fast a truncation converges: Eq. (7)
            converges at ν = 2 because Stillinger–Weber is three-body, while Tersoff’s bond order spreads over every order (Figure 2).
            That is the case for nonlinear embeddings (Eq. 8) and, one step further, for message passing on tree graphs, where GRACE builds
            high effective orders from low-order layers (<a href="/models/grace/">GRACE</a>). The self-interaction terms make the
            lower-order coefficients absorb parts of higher-order physics, which changes conditioning but not the span
            <Cite id="dusson2022ace" locator="Eq. (4.4); Sec. 8, Table 2" />.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="out-h">
      <h2 id="out-h" class="h-section">Outputs</h2>
      <p>
        Energy, with forces and stresses as its derivatives; in pacemaker also the extrapolation grade per atom for active learning
        <Cite id="lysogorskiy2023active" locator="Sec. III.2" />. MD runs in LAMMPS through the ML-PACE package. There are no charges
        or electrostatics.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="fail-h">
      <h2 id="fail-h" class="h-section">Where it can fail</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>From the architecture.</strong> Nothing beyond the cutoff exists: no Coulomb tails, image charges or LO–TO splitting
        (<a href="/physics-lab/ewald/">P6</a>; <a href="/failures/electrostatics-at-electrodes/">Failure Atlas</a>). A polynomial basis is
        unconstrained outside the data, and Figure 2’s dimers and compressed cells show how far a fit can fall when it has never seen a
        short bond; this is why pacemaker adds a core repulsion and ACEpotentials.jl a repulsive pair envelope
        <Cite id="witt2023acepotentials" locator="Eq. (7)" />, which only help if they are strong enough at the distances reached. The basis
        grows quickly with order and degree (Figure 1), and its size multiplies with the number of species combinations. A target that
        depends nonlinearly on all neighbours at once converges slowly in body order unless the model’s nonlinearity matches it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>From a fitted model.</strong> An ACE potential inherits its reference data and functional: the Cu PACE is a PBE copper
        model fitted to about {cuData.size?.structures.toLocaleString('en')} FHI-aims calculations <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. II.1" />,
        and its two densities are fitted quantities, not electron densities (Figure 3). The fits are system-specific: the Cu and Si
        potentials do not transfer to other elements, and their accuracy figures (2.9 meV/atom for Cu near the ground state, 1.81 meV/atom
        for Si within 1 eV) hold for their own test sets <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. II.2" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="ck-h">
      <h2 id="ck-h" class="h-section">Checkpoints</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="ck">
          <thead><tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Basis</th><th>Embedding</th><th>Cutoff</th><th>Training data</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>{cu.name}</td>
              <td>{s.total_functions} functions, ν ≤ {s.rankmax}, l ≤ {s.lmax}; {cu.parameters?.total?.toLocaleString('en')} parameters (paper)</td>
              <td>ρ₁ + F(ρ₂), m = {s.densities[1]!.m}</td>
              <td>{s.cutoff} Å</td>
              <td>{cuData.name}</td>
              <td>{cu.licence}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        The paper reports 756 expansion coefficients per density <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" locator="Sec. II.2" />; the released file
        holds {s.total_functions} basis functions, each with one coefficient per density. The Si potential of the same paper
        (<code>Si_npj_CompMat2021.ace</code>, same Zenodo record) is not yet recorded here. Foundation-scale ACE descendants are on the
        <a href="/models/grace/">GRACE page</a>.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Related families">
        <a href="/models/mtp/">Moment tensor potentials</a> span the same space with Cartesian tensors and introduced the extrapolation
        grade. <a href="/models/gap/">GAP</a> uses the ν = 2 SOAP subset with a kernel. <a href="/models/mace/">MACE</a> builds ACE-like
        products inside message passing, and <a href="/models/grace/">GRACE</a> generalises ACE from stars to trees.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="res-h">
      <h2 id="res-h" class="h-section">Resources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The original expansion: <Cite id="drautz2019ace" />.</li>
        <li>Completeness, the symmetric basis and its evaluation: <Cite id="dusson2022ace" />.</li>
        <li>PACE, the Cu and Si potentials and the recursive evaluator: <Cite id="lysogorskiy2021pace" />; pacemaker: <Cite id="bochkarev2022pacemaker" />; active learning: <Cite id="lysogorskiy2023active" />.</li>
        <li>ACEpotentials.jl, sparsification and smoothness priors: <Cite id="witt2023acepotentials" />.</li>
        <li>Code: <a href="https://github.com/ICAMS/python-ace">python-ace / pacemaker</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ICAMS/lammps-user-pace">LAMMPS ML-PACE</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ACEsuit/ACEpotentials.jl">ACEpotentials.jl</a>; released Cu and Si potentials: <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4734036">Zenodo 4734036</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
